[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the question I flagged: [1mwho changes the task?[0m

  In a client-only app there's one browser and one person, so by default every change is your own — and a notification
  system that tells you what you just did is noise, not a feature. Whatever we pick here determines whether an [38;5;153mactorId[39m
  exists on every event, which is a field the eventual backend will certainly need. Adding it later means backfilling
  every stored event.

  - [1mSeeded users + "acting as" switcher[0m — the app ships with two or three demo users and a header control for who you
    currently are. Changes are attributed to the active user; you get notified about changes [3mothers[0m made to tasks you
    care about. Buys a feature that's genuinely demonstrable and testable today, and forces the actor modeling the
    backend will need anyway. Costs: a user-switcher UI that a real multi-user version would throw away, and per-user
    notification state in storage.
  - [1mSingle user, notify on everything including your own edits[0m — simplest possible. Buys the least code. Costs: the core
    UX is wrong from day one, self-suppression has to be retrofitted, and you can't tell whether the "care about" logic
    actually works because everything notifies.
  - [1mSingle user, suppress self-changes[0m — correct semantics, but nothing ever fires until there's a backend. Buys
    honesty. Costs: you ship a notification system you cannot see work, and every test has to fabricate a fake actor
    anyway — at which point you've built the switcher's guts without its UI.

  [1mMy recommendation: the seeded users + switcher.[0m The third option's hidden cost is the decisive one: you need a second
  actor to test [3many[0m of this, so you're building actor support regardless. The switcher just exposes what you already
  have, and the throwaway cost is one small header control.
